  "narrative": "This batch resolves several key confounding questions and sharpens the interpretation of the dominant supplied-dataset associations. The most important result is H107: among cells that are wild type for TP53, KEAP1, and SMARCA4 and fall in the high mutation-burden range, MSI-high is associated with a large signed difference of +0.731 [0.649, 0.812] on the claimed negative direction, corresponding to a raw lower dependency score of about -0.73 for MSI-high cells. The exposed and comparator cells are valid and sufficiently large, and the interval remains far above the 0.15-unit threshold. I accept H107 and keep it active while requesting independent validation, because it is the most decisive burden-restricted molecular-clean test of the MSI-high association. It does not prove full independence from mutation-burden biology, because it still restricts to the burden range where MSI-high occurs, but it makes the association harder to attribute solely to the strongest co-occurring losses or to a global high-burden state.\n\nH105 and H106 further support MSI-high stability within the available burden strata: +0.818 [0.668, 0.969] in the median-to-upper quartile burden interval and +0.745 [0.583, 0.907] in the upper-quartile burden stratum. Both are large, valid, and far above threshold. I accept and close them as burden-interval qualifications. H118 tests whether MSI-high differs between high-ploidy and lower-ploidy cells. The signed interaction estimate is +0.103 [-0.1196, 0.3265], below the 0.15-unit threshold and compatible with no meaningful interaction. I reject and close it, noting that ploidy is not shown to modify the MSI-high association in these data.\n\nThe established dominant molecular associations remain strong in contexts that could otherwise attenuate or confound them. In high mutation-burden cells, TP53 loss, KEAP1 loss, and SMARCA4 loss retain very large effects: TP53-loss signed +0.823 [0.735, 0.911], KEAP1-loss +0.783 [0.686, 0.880], and SMARCA4-loss +0.847 [0.755, 0.939]. Within co-occurring loss contexts, SMARCA4 loss within KEAP1 loss is +0.814 [0.684, 0.945], TP53 loss within SMARCA4 loss is +0.897 [0.789, 1.005], and KEAP1 loss within SMARCA4 loss is +0.790 [0.661, 0.920]. These are far above threshold, so I accept and close them as completed qualification results. The global TP53, KEAP1, and SMARCA4 associations are therefore not artifacts of overlap with MSI-high, high mutation burden, or the absence/presence of other dominant losses in the tested contexts. All statements remain associations in this supplied dataset, not causal or generalizable claims.\n\nThe moderate PTEN association is the main source of residual uncertainty. In high mutation-burden cells, the signed estimate is +0.196 [0.058, 0.333], and in SMARCA4-loss cells it is +0.157 [-0.030, 0.344]. Both point estimates are above or near threshold, but the intervals include values that are not scientifically meaningful and even include zero. I therefore leave H116 and H109 unresolved and deferred rather than accepting them as independent conditional PTEN claims. This does not overturn the accepted global PTEN association, but it confirms that PTEN effect size is threshold-boundary and context-sensitive.\n\nI request validation for H107 rather than H105 or H106 because H107 combines the cleanest molecular restriction with the relevant burden range and therefore most directly tests whether the MSI-high lower-dependency association survives removal of the strongest molecular confounders within the only burden range in which MSI-high occurs.",
  "research_notes": "Current accepted supplied-dataset associations: TP53 loss is associated with markedly higher dependency_composite, about +0.83 signed/raw units; KEAP1 loss about +0.81 higher; SMARCA4 loss about -0.80 raw lower; MSI-high about -0.75 to -0.78 raw lower globally, in triple TP53/KEAP1/SMARCA4 wild-type, in high mutation burden, in median-to-upper burden, and in upper-quartile burden. PTEN loss is a moderate lower-dependency association about -0.24 to -0.25 raw units, accepted globally but threshold-boundary and context-dependent.\n\nNew qualification results: H113-H115 show TP53, KEAP1, and SMARCA4 effects remain large in high mutation burden. H110-H112 show strong effects in loss co-occurrence contexts. H105-H107 show MSI-high remains large across burden ranges; H107 is clean triple-wild-type plus high-burden and is accepted active pending validation. H118 rejects MSI-high by ploidy interaction. H116 and H109 leave PTEN conditional effects unresolved because intervals cross threshold and often zero.\n\nRemaining uncertainties: (1) whether H107 replicates under independent validation; (2) whether PTEN has a robust conditional effect beyond global moderate association; (3) whether MSI-high is separable from mutation-burden biology in any deeper sense. Registered next priorities include H117, other MSI library/lineage/loss-context tests, and pending TP53/KEAP1 interactions with ploidy or mutation burden if they become informative. Lower-priority unresolved main tests for RB1, BRCA2, APC, SMAD4, EGFR/ERBB2/BRAF, research markers, libraries, omics, and media remain available but are less central unless they reveal new large effects. New proposals register clean-context TP53 and KEAP1 tests and a PTEN triple-wild-type MSI-low refinement to further assess separability and threshold stability. All conclusions are dataset associations, not causal or external generalizations.",
